course: dictionary
lesson: 2774
title: Disease
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A disease is an illness that affects people, animals or plants, and it usually has signs called symptoms. «Bats can carry rabies, a disease that is deadly once symptoms start but can be prevented with injections given soon after a bite.» The sentence tells you rabies is a disease, and that a disease can be prevented.
explain: Read the words around disease to work out what it means. «After 1492 diseases such as smallpox, carried by Europeans, killed very many native people of the Americas.» Diseases are illnesses that can spread and harm many people. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "illnesses such as smallpox" still makes sense.
explain: Disease can also mean illness in general, with no a in front. «Crowded pens can spread sea lice and disease to wild fish.» Here disease is not one named illness. It is illness as a whole, which can spread from fish to fish.
fact: A disease is an illness that affects people, animals or plants.
fact: Some diseases are infectious: they are caused by germs and can spread, like flu, measles and chickenpox.
fact: Other diseases are not infectious, like diabetes.
fact: We fight disease with handwashing, clean water, healthy food, exercise and vaccines.
fact: Plants can get diseases too, like mould on leaves.
exercise: «Bats can carry rabies, a disease that is deadly once symptoms start.» What is a disease? | A healthy food | A clean drink of water | *An illness that affects people, animals or plants | A kind of bat
exercise: «Crowded pens can spread sea lice and disease to wild fish.» What does spread disease mean here? | Catch a fish in a net | *Pass an illness from one fish to another | Make the fish bigger | Clean the water in the sea
exercise: Which of these is an infectious disease, caused by germs? | Diabetes | *Measles | A sore arm after a jab | Clean water
test: Plants can get diseases too. Which is an example? | Sunshine | A bird's nest | Smooth pebbles | *Mould on leaves
test: «A sore arm after a jab is a normal sign the immune system is responding, and does not mean the vaccine gave you the disease.» What does this tell you? | A sore arm means you have the disease | *A sore arm is not the same as getting the disease | Jabs are only for plants | Vaccines cause disease on purpose
exam: Which is one way we fight disease? | Ignoring symptoms | *Handwashing | Staying dirty | Eating sweets all day
